cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this research record, please cite it as below."
title: "CompositionProof-2: Cross-Witness Correlation Across Quantum Hardware Calibration Epochs - Preregistered Protocol and Epoch 1 Evidence"
type: dataset
authors:
  - given-names: Derek
    family-names: Hone
    affiliation: "Remnant Fieldworks Inc."
abstract: >-
  Preregistered, longitudinal hardware research campaign. The design, sampling
  plan, correlation estimator, effect band, drift guards, and permitted-claim
  wording were frozen and SHA-256 hash-locked before any quantum hardware ran.
  The campaign tests whether the epoch-to-epoch fluctuations of two independently
  defined nonclassicality witnesses (CHSH and Mermin-3) covary across distinct
  hardware calibration states on one named IBM device. This release documents the
  frozen protocol and its first collected calibration epoch (Epoch 1): executed on
  ibm_marrakesh (IBM Heron r2, 156 qubits), CHSH S=2.7205, Mermin-3 |M|=3.7710,
  11 QPU-seconds. Epoch 1 is ONE observation in a preregistered series requiring
  at least 20 distinct calibration epochs (target 30). No longitudinal
  CORRELATED / UNCORRELATED / INCONCLUSIVE verdict is reported or implied by this
  release; a single epoch has no fluctuation series. Governance: each hardware
  epoch requires fresh authorization (prior authorization does not constitute
  current execution permission). Honesty bounds: device-dependent witnesses on
  co-located qubits; locality and detection loopholes OPEN; NOT device-independent
  and NOT loophole-free; any future cross-epoch correlation is an observed
  association on one device over the measured window, NOT a causal common cause
  and NOT universal; detecting correlation is reliable at n~30 but certifying its
  absence needs n~150; no first/only claim beyond cited DOIs.
keywords:
  - quantum
  - nonclassicality witness
  - CHSH
  - Mermin inequality
  - proof composition
  - calibration drift
  - longitudinal
  - preregistration
  - reproducibility
  - ExecutionProof
  - Coherence-Inheritance Fusion
